WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:07.540
So, the first part of our optimization tutorial is going to talk about optimization philosophy

2
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:14.380
because there are some things that you need to keep in mind when optimizing any Unreal

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.219
Engine project.

4
00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:21.059
The first thing is that optimization isn't a stage in your project.

5
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:24.500
It is something that is started at the beginning of your project and then carried

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.300
out through the lifetime of your project.

7
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.350
Optimization should also be something that you do post-project for DLCs or any bug fixes

8
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:36.259
or things like that that you might need to apply to your project.

9
00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:42.520
Now when you are working with optimization, you need to communicate budgets with your

10
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.820
team and there's also going to be no single magic setting or switch that will instantly

11
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.460
improve performance.

12
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.060
Real performance gains are going to come from small updates and refinements that all

13
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.450
work together to give you steady performance.

14
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:02.890
Performance should also be treated like a feature and not an afterthought.

15
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.750
That means that during prototyping, development, and polish, performance should be thought

16
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.159
about just like any other production-ready feature.

17
00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:14.680
Performance is also everybody's responsibility.

18
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.620
You should be testing performance early on, you should be testing it often and consistently

19
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.780
and also always on your target platforms.

20
00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:27.560
Again, remember, there shouldn't just be a stage where, okay, now we're going to get

21
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.920
to optimization and performance.

22
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.760
This is something that needs to be done through the lifetime of your project.

23
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.890
One of the other things that can help is defining good performance goals, but this

24
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:50.920
has a caveat of many times those require you asking questions like, how many triangles

25
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.060
or meshes can exist on screen at one time?

26
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.140
And the problem with that is that those numbers don't necessarily transfer from project

27
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.140
to project.

28
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.950
What worked on one project might not necessarily be the best for what works on another project.

29
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.949
A better way is to build the scene so that it looks like you want and then look for

30
00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:14.569
opportunities to optimize.

31
00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.889
Avoid setting strict limits such as there can only be 10 characters on screen at one

32
00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:27.530
time and instead figure out ways to get past these with clever optimization and content

33
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:34.680
reduction in other areas so that you can have what you want without sacrificing performance

34
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.099
in other areas.

35
00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:41.759
A couple of general best practices with performance just to keep in mind is that build

36
00:02:41.759 --> 00:02:48.319
in a way that avoids common performance issues such as unnecessary math on each frame, keeping

37
00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:53.740
texture sizes within reasonable limits and reduce the number of shading bends when possible,

38
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.379
and use instancing to improve rendering efficiency.

39
00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:03.599
When we are trying to optimize builds based on profiling, we want to make sure that we

40
00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:08.400
focus on improvements that are identified through profiling data.

41
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.759
We also want to try and avoid blindly optimizing things.

42
00:03:14.759 --> 00:03:21.139
For example, if we know that materials can be an issue, but we don't know if they're

43
00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:25.680
an issue on our project, don't go in and start optimizing all of your materials.

44
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.419
Make sure that you're using the profiling tools to determine if materials are an issue

45
00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:34.409
or not and then go from there.

46
00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:41.580
Remember, use the CPU and GPU profiling tools to identify specific bottlenecks and then

47
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.300
focus on those bottlenecks instead of making wild performance changes in areas that might

48
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.199
not necessarily benefit from doing performance work.

49
00:03:52.199 --> 00:03:58.139
And finally, just to reiterate, remember that optimization and performance is something

50
00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:00.620
that needs to be done throughout the lifetime of your project.

51
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:06.319
From the project start to the project end, everyone should be responsible for and always

52
00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:11.580
looking at and trying to maintain the performance of any Unreal Engine 5 project they're working

53
00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:12.080
on.
